A follow-up on your second point, about a stable identity travelling with the message.

Half of it is already proposed in #65899: a stamp implementing `PropagatedStampInterface` is copied onto every message dispatched while the message carrying it is handled, at any depth and across buses. That pull request now also ships a `CorrelationStamp` built on it, so the flow identifier is part of the component rather than something each application reinvents.

I checked what that gives here, with the two branches merged and a correlation stamp attached to the command that opens the flow:

```
1. stored in the outbox: 1, trace=abc-123, outbox target=orders
2. forwarded to the target: 1, trace=abc-123, outbox stamp=stripped
3. handled by the consumer: 1
```

The stamp is copied onto the event the command handler emits, stored in the outbox row with it, forwarded to the broker with it, and reaches the consumer. Neither branch knows about the other, and they touch disjoint files, so either can land first. The one requirement is that such a stamp is sendable, since it is serialized twice on that path.

What remains of your point is therefore narrower than I wrote earlier: a per-message identifier and the causation link. Causation is not plain propagation, since a child inherits the correlation unchanged but takes the parent's message id as its cause, so it needs its own derivation step. That is the piece I would build next, on top of #65899 rather than here.
